  "text": "Certainly! You're looking to write a function that creates a **word histogram**, which is a dictionary that counts how often each word appears in a string, ignoring **case** and **punctuation**.\n\nHere's how you can do it in Python:\n\n### \u2705 Steps:\n1. Convert the string to **lowercase** to ensure case-insensitive matching.\n2. **Remove punctuation** \u2014 you can use the `string` module or `re` module for this.\n3. Split the string into words.\n4. Use a dictionary to count the frequency of each word.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Here's the code:\n\n```python\nimport string\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequencies\n    histogram = defaultdict(int)\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] += 1\n    \n    # Convert defaultdict to regular dict for the final result\n    return dict(histogram)\n```\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Let's test it with your sample inputs:\n\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccc Notes:\n- We used `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` to remove all punctuation.\n- We used `defaultdict` for counting, then converted it to `dict` for better readability and compatibility with your assertions.\n\nLet me know if you want a version without using `collections.defaultdict`!",
